// DM mission files: the opening, "On Your Bike" (missions 1–12). Checks: [stat or 'a|b', band, when, pass, fail].
export default {
  m01: {
    brief: 'The crew are skint, the rent is due Friday and Auntie Pat wants two bikes by tonight. This is the tutorial with teeth: the lock roll, the first time somebody watches, and the first sale at The Arches. Underneath it, Pat is quietly testing them. She wants to see whether they come back with clean bikes and clean stories, or with trouble on their tail. One of the bikes they nick belongs to a Lea Boy, and that is the first loose thread of the whole campaign.',
    open: 'Somebody is hammering on the door like they owe him money. They do. Mo from Chicken Palace stands on the landing in his whites, smelling of fryer oil and disappointment. "Friday," he says, holding up his pen like a warning. "Rent. Friday. And Auntie Pat wants a word. Today, she says. Not Friday. Today."\n\nYour Blower buzzes on the counter, the screen cracked right across Pat\'s smiling face. Two bikes. Tonight. Nothing fancy. Kez will show you where.',
    cast: ['mo-rahman', 'kez'],
    roles: {'auntie-pat': 'Gives the order and buys the bikes at The Arches. Watches how the crew talk about it afterwards.', 'spanner-rob': 'Strips the bikes at the Chainring Storehouse and spots anything that will come back on them.', 'mo-rahman': 'The landlord at the door. Wants Friday\'s rent and no police on his stairs.', kez: 'Texts the tip-offs: which rack, which lock, how long until the owner comes out.'},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'AR4', title: 'Pat\'s office, under the arches', read: 'The Arches smell of chain oil and burnt coffee. Trains rattle overhead every four minutes and nobody flinches any more. Pat sits behind a desk built from an old workbench, reading glasses on, counting twenty-pound notes like she is dealing cards. She does not look up until she has finished the pile. "Two bikes, darlings. Road bikes if you can, anything with gears if you can\'t. Bring them here, not to anyone else, and I\'ll see you right."',
       text: 'Pat gives the order in person or on the Blower (Ask Pat for an order). She explains her one rule plainly: nobody gets hurt, nobody gets followed home. If the players ask about money, she names the rough price for a decent road bike (about a third of what it is worth) and the mission award on top.',
       checks: [['sav', 'easy', 'Read Pat: what is she really asking?', 'She is sizing you up. Clean work now buys bigger orders later; she says as much with her eyebrows.', 'She is just a nice old lady who buys bikes. (She isn\'t.)']]},
      {at: 'LF1', title: 'The rack outside London Fields Lido', read: 'Saturday at London Fields: buggies, spaniels, a man selling flat whites from a converted horsebox. Outside the Lido a charity cycle ride has left a whole rack of tasty bikes, chained in a hurry, the owners inside doing lengths in the cold water. Kez leans on his BMX across the road, pretends to be on his phone, and gives you a tiny nod at the third one along.',
       text: 'Street Life puts chained bikes on the map; Kez\'s tip-off bikes are a map or two away. The lock roll is Graft (the difficulty comes from the lock). This is the moment to teach Heat: anyone watching turns a nicked bike into one star. Halfway through the second lock, a woman in a club jersey comes out of LF1.2, towel round her neck, and clocks her own bike in your hands.',
       checks: [['grf', 'medium', 'Cut the lock before anyone comes out', 'Click, and it\'s yours. Ride it, don\'t run it.', 'The lock jams or the grinder shrieks. Someone starts filming.'],
         ['sav', 'medium', 'Spot the Old Bill before you start', 'A patrol car idling by the Pub on the Park. Wait two minutes and it rolls off.', 'You only see it when you\'re already elbow-deep in the lock: witnessed, one star.'],
         ['frt|mov', 'medium', 'The owner in the club jersey: talk your way out (Front) or leg it (Moves)', 'Front: "Council bike marking scheme, love." She actually thanks you. Moves: gone across the grass before her flip-flops are on.', 'She has a whistle and a very loud voice. One star, and she remembers your face.']]},
      {at: 'HC7.2', title: 'The Chainring Storehouse', read: 'Round the back of Morning Lane, behind a roller door with someone else\'s company name on it, is the Chainring Storehouse. Spanner Rob looks up from a stand where a bike is being taken apart so fast it is almost a magic trick. He takes one look at your haul and puts down his tea. "This one\'s nice," he says. "This one\'s trouble." Then a bloke at the roller door shouts that the second bike is his.',
       text: 'Spanner can make a bike unrecognisable (new bar tape, swapped saddle, the frame number filed). The shouting bloke is a Lea Boy\'s cousin. If the crew handle him badly he gets his phone out and photographs everyone.',
       checks: [['grf', 'hard', 'Help Spanner make the second bike unrecognisable before the bloke gets a photo', 'Bar tape, saddle, transfers peeled: it is just a bike now. He walks off muttering.', 'He gets a photo of the frame and one of you. Stretch will see it by midnight.'],
         ['btl', 'medium', 'Stare the bloke down at the roller door', 'He decides he was mistaken and goes home to think about it.', 'He calls his cousin. Two mopeds circle the block twenty minutes later.']]}
    ],
    close: 'Pat turns the first bike upside down, spins the wheel, listens to it the way other people listen to music. "Lovely." Twenty-pound notes appear on the desk. Then she looks at the second bike for a long moment, and at you for slightly longer. "Next time," she says, "find out whose it is before you take it. Saves everyone a lot of bother." Overhead, a train goes by. The rent is paid. For now.',
    notes: ['Run it light: this mission teaches the systems. Let the players try the lock roll, see Heat appear, and pull up at The Arches to get Pat\'s whispered offer with its Sell button.',
      'Kez is the tutorial voice: if the players stall, he texts a hint ("bolt cutters make Hard locks Medium, angle grinder makes them Easy but LOUD").',
      'The Lea Boy\'s bike is the hook for Mission 3 and Mission 4. Note which PC was seen with it.',
      'If they fail badly, don\'t end the job: a short-changed Pat is still a buyer. She just remembers.']
  },
  m02: {
    brief: 'Pat has a buyer for a Carbon Whippet road bike (about £1,800 new). There are three Whippets on the rack at Broadway Market. One is real, one is a police bait bike with a tracker in the seat post (DS Vale\'s), and one was already sold twice by a fixer. Then the buyers compete: a private viewing at The Spurstowe Arms and a collector at the Highbury Barn Tavern with an envelope. The real test is greed. A secret switch pays now and bites later.',
    open: 'Broadway Market on a Saturday is a riot of sourdough, vintage leather and people who say "amazing" about coffee. Outside the café with the queue round the corner, three Carbon Whippets lean against the rack like racehorses tied to a lamppost. Matte black, deep-section wheels, each worth more than everything in your flat put together. Kez texts: "white bar tape is the real one. prob. lol."',
    cast: ['kez', 'spanner-rob', 'harriet-vale'],
    roles: {'auntie-pat': 'Has the first buyer and expects the Whippet at The Arches.', 'felix-dyer': 'The barista who watches the rack and knows the owners\' habits.', 'mina-dey': 'The letting clerk who already sold the same bike to two people.', kez: 'Scouts the rack, sends the (half-right) tip.', 'spanner-rob': 'Tests the bike in the Chop Bay and finds the tracker if there is one.', 'harriet-vale': 'Off stage: her bait bike is on the rack.'},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'EX-BROADWAY', title: 'The coffee rack', read: 'The barista in the window, a lad with a bleached buzz cut, is watching the rack more than he is watching the milk. Every time someone comes out with a cup, he looks at the bikes. When he sees you looking at the bikes, he looks at you.',
       text: 'Three Whippets. Felix the barista knows which one belongs to the regular who never locks the front wheel. The bait bike is newest and shiniest, with a fat seat post (the tracker). The third has a sold sticker from a "private sale" (Mina\'s handiwork).',
       checks: [['sav', 'hard', 'Tell the three Whippets apart (spot the bait bike)', 'The fat seat post and the too-new lock: bait. Leave it. Take the one with white tape.', 'You take the bait. A unit will turn up at The Arches next session (and Vale has a new file).'],
         ['frt', 'medium', 'Get Felix the barista talking', 'He tells you which owner sits at the back for forty minutes and never locks the front wheel.', 'He gets twitchy and texts someone. The owner comes out early.'],
         ['grf', 'hard', 'The lock (bolt cutters make it Medium, the grinder Easy but loud)', 'Clean cut, ride away down the canal towpath, bikes only, no police cars.', 'A shriek of metal and forty phones come up. Witnessed: one star.']]},
      {at: 'LF4.2', title: 'The buyer at The Spurstowe Arms', read: 'The snug at The Spurstowe smells of crisps and wet dog. Pat\'s buyer is a pale man in a gilet who keeps saying "super" and has brought a mate with a cycling cap and a torch. The mate does not say anything. He just shines the torch at the bottom bracket and frowns.',
       text: 'The buyer wants a look before he pays. His mate knows bikes and looks for the frame number. If the bike is the bait, the mate finds the tracker and the buyer walks.',
       checks: [['frt', 'medium', 'Sell it to the buyer and his mate', 'He pays Pat\'s price and a bit for your trouble. "Super."', 'The mate finds a scratch, a story doesn\'t add up, the price drops by a third.']]},
      {at: 'HB4.2', title: 'The collector at the Highbury Barn Tavern', read: 'In the snug at the Barn Tavern, a man in a camel coat slides a fat envelope across the table without opening his mouth. It is more than Pat\'s buyer is paying. Much more. Then the door opens behind you and Pat\'s buyer walks in with his mate and his torch.',
       text: 'This is the greed test. Selling to the collector pays now and costs Pat\'s trust (and the first buyer tells people). Mina Dey is here too, trying to explain why she sold the same bike to the collector last week.',
       checks: [['btl', 'medium', 'Hold your nerve with both buyers in the room', 'You pick one, calmly, and the other leaves without a scene.', 'Voices go up, the landlord calls time on all of you, and both buyers think you\'re a mug.'],
         ['mov', 'medium', 'Leg it with the bike if it goes wrong', 'Out the kitchen passage and away.', 'You get out. The bike doesn\'t.']]}
    ],
    close: 'Spanner puts the Whippet on the stand, spins the cranks, and holds the seat post up to the light. If there is a tracker, it is in his hand now, blinking a tiny red light at you like an accusation. If there isn\'t, he just nods. "Nice," he says, which from Spanner is a standing ovation.',
    notes: ['The tracker is the big consequence: if they took the bait bike and it reached The Arches, flag a police visit next session (it feeds LD1 and Mission 7).',
      'Broadway Market and the coffee rack are on the Bethnal Green map; the objective "Get to Broadway Market" ticks on Haggerston or Bethnal Green.',
      'If the players think to ask Felix, reward it: information is cheaper than a failed lock.',
      'Selling to the collector is legitimate play. Pat finds out (she always does) and the next order is smaller.']
  },
  m03: {
    brief: 'The Lea Boys have driven off with Pat\'s van of stripped frames from the Loading Yard. The van goes east along Morning Lane and Wick Road for Bow Breakers, but one crate has already been booked onward to the Orient Night Shed under a company nobody has heard of: the first thread of the Quiet Ledger. Stretch waits on the Hertford Union bridge to negotiate, or fight. The choice of grassing him to DS Vale is offered, and Pat hates grasses.',
    open: 'Pat is standing in the middle of an empty loading yard holding a mug of tea she has forgotten about. There is a rectangle of clean tarmac where her van was. "Frames," she says, very quietly. "Forty frames, stripped and boxed. And my van. It was the Lea Boys, darlings. Kez saw the tracksuits." She puts the mug down. "I would like my van back before it crosses the Lea."',
    cast: ['stretch', 'lea-boy', 'kez', 'harriet-vale'],
    roles: {'auntie-pat': 'Wants the van and every frame back. Will not forgive a grass.', 'ada-finch': 'The sound artist at The Adam and Eve who heard the van go and recorded it.', 'marisol-vega': 'Runs the night shift at the Orient Night Shed and can count crates.', stretch: 'Waits on the bridge to bargain or brawl.', 'lea-boy': 'Drives the van and is not paid enough for a chase.', kez: 'Saw the tracksuits and which way they went.', 'harriet-vale': 'The phone number the crew could ring. It costs them Pat.'},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'AR5', title: 'The Loading Yard', read: 'Oil stain, tyre marks, a smashed padlock on the gate. A neighbour\'s CCTV camera points straight at the yard, red light blinking. Kez rolls in on his BMX, breathless: "White Tranzit, two of Stretch\'s, heading for Morning Lane. Five minutes ago. Maybe six."',
       text: 'They need wheels, now. Steal something fast, or borrow the police area car on Mare Street (stealing a police vehicle: two stars straight away).',
       checks: [['grf', 'medium', 'Get some wheels, fast (any motor on the street)', 'Wired and away in under a minute.', 'Forty seconds of swearing at a steering column. The van has a lead.'],
         ['sav', 'easy', 'Work out where they\'re going', 'East along Morning Lane, Wick Road, Waterden: Bow Breakers, or across the Lea.', 'You guess wrong for one map before Kez corrects you.']]},
      {at: 'WW3.2', title: 'The Adam and Eve', read: 'A woman with a shaved head and a field recorder round her neck is sitting outside The Adam and Eve with half a lager. "White van? Slipping fan belt? Went past three minutes ago doing about sixty." She holds up her recorder. "I\'ve got it if you want to hear it."',
       text: 'Ada Finch recorded the van. Buying her a drink buys the route (and later, the recording is evidence: someone in the van said a company name).',
       checks: [['frt', 'easy', 'Get Ada to share the route (and the recording)', 'She plays it back: two Lea Boys arguing, and the words "Orient Way, the night shed". That is where one crate is going.', 'She gives you the road but keeps the recording for her art.']]},
      {at: 'EX-WATERDEN', title: 'The chase: Morning Lane to Waterden Road', read: 'There it is: the white Tranzit, back doors tied with rope, swinging through the junction at Wick Road like it is on rails. The driver sees you in his mirror. The van lurches and goes for the gap between a bus and a skip lorry.',
       text: 'Run this as a turn-based chase (start one from Street Control or the Combat Console), or as opposed Wheels rolls: a win closes the gap, a loss opens it; boxed in at 0, gone at 5. A brick through the van\'s windscreen from a passenger seat is Aim.',
       checks: [['whl', 'opposed', 'Close the gap on the van (each round)', 'You box it in at the Waterden roundabout.', 'It pulls away; one more loss and it is across the Lea.'],
         ['aim', 'hard', 'Throw something through its windscreen from the passenger seat', 'Crazed glass: the driver panics and stops on the verge.', 'It bounces off and you nearly lose a hand.'],
         ['grt', 'medium', 'Brace for the crunch if you ram it', 'Bruised but driving.', 'Hurt, and the airbag smells like burnt hair.']]},
      {at: 'EX-BREAKERS', title: 'Bow Breakers and the bridge', read: 'On the Hertford Union bridge, Stretch is leaning on the railings like he is waiting for a bus. Two Lea Boys stand behind him, hoods up. "Pat\'s gone soft," he says, pleasantly. "You know it, I know it. Let\'s talk numbers, or let\'s not talk."',
       text: 'Stretch offers money for half the frames and a "partnership". The crew can pay him off, fight (Combat Console; Stretch and two lads), or ring DS Vale. One crate has already gone on to the Orient Night Shed (HM7.2), booked under Orchard Lease.',
       checks: [['mus', 'medium', 'If it kicks off: shove Stretch\'s lads off the bridge path', 'They go down and stay down.', 'You\'re the one on the floor.'],
         ['btl', 'hard', 'Face Stretch down without blinking', 'He laughs and tells you which crate went to the night shed. "Call it a gift."', 'He knows you\'re scared. The price of the frames doubles.']]}
    ],
    close: 'The van comes home with one crate missing and a delivery note in the glovebox that nobody at The Arches has ever seen before: Orchard Lease, care of the Orient Night Shed, signature illegible. Pat reads it twice and folds it into her cardigan pocket. "That," she says, "is not the Lea Boys\' handwriting."',
    notes: ['Grassing Stretch to DS Vale works (half his crew get lifted) but Pat hears about it. She does not like grasses, even useful ones. Lower her opinion of the crew and remember it in Mission 8.',
      'The missing crate and the Orchard Lease note point to HB8 Orchard Lease Office: the first thread of the Quiet Ledger. Leave it hanging.',
      'A stolen police area car is a two-star crime on its own, before the chase. That is a lot of Heat for a tutorial; let them feel it.',
      'If they lose the van entirely, the frames turn up for sale at Bow Breakers next week. Big Den will sell them back, at a price.']
  },
  m04: {
    brief: 'Three in the morning, the Lea Boys hit The Arches: mopeds outside, a brick through Pat\'s window, and petrol poured under the shutter. It is a fight in the yard and a fire risk. The real story is that the petrol was not Stretch\'s idea. Somebody paid for it, and somebody told Stretch where Pat keeps her ledger. There is a grass close to Pat.',
    open: 'Your Blower lights up the ceiling at 3:07am. It\'s Kez, and he isn\'t texting, he\'s calling, and he never calls. "They\'re at the Arches. Mopeds. Loads. Pat\'s inside." Behind him you hear glass going and an engine revving, then the line goes dead.\n\nBy the time you round the corner onto Morning Lane, the smell of petrol is so thick you can taste it.',
    cast: ['auntie-pat', 'kez', 'lea-boy', 'spanner-rob'],
    roles: {'auntie-pat': 'Inside the office with the ledger, a fire extinguisher and no intention of leaving.', kez: 'Rang the alarm. Hiding behind the bins with a bloody nose.', 'lea-boy': 'Four of them in the yard; one has a petrol bomb.', 'spanner-rob': 'Arrives in his slippers with a tyre lever.'},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'AR5', title: 'The yard at 3am', read: 'Four mopeds idle in the yard with their lights off. Four lads in black, faces under snoods. One of them is crouched at the shutter with a jerry can, petrol running in a dark river under the door. Another has a bottle with a rag in the neck and a lighter in his other hand. He sees you, and grins.',
       text: 'Start a fight in the Combat Console: four Lea Boys (GM Screen → NPCs → Spawn, twice). The petrol bomb is a hazard. Whoever is nearest the shutter is in trouble. Police arrive after three rounds if anyone fires a gun.',
       checks: [['aim', 'hard', 'Knock the lighter or the bottle out of his hand with a thrown spanner', 'The bottle smashes on the cobbles, unlit. Petrol and no fire.', 'It goes up against the shutter. Fire and Smoke for anyone close.'],
         ['mus', 'medium', 'Hold the shutter while Pat gets the extinguisher', 'The shutter holds; the fire dies in the doorway.', 'It buckles: smoke inside the arches.'],
         ['grt', 'medium', 'Get through the smoke to Pat', 'Coughing, eyes streaming, you get to her.', 'Knackered, and the smoke has the ledger room.']]},
      {at: 'AR4', title: 'The one left standing', read: 'The last Lea Boy is sitting on an upturned crate, holding his ribs, snood pulled down. He is younger than you thought. Nineteen, maybe. He looks at Pat, then at you, then at the floor.',
       text: 'The lad talks if handled right: it was Stretch who sent them, but somebody told Stretch where Pat keeps the ledger, and somebody else paid for the petrol (cash, through a man none of them knew). Somebody close to Pat is talking.',
       checks: [['frt', 'medium', 'Get him talking (gently, or not)', 'Stretch sent them. A stranger paid for the petrol. And Stretch knew where the ledger was.', 'He gives you Stretch\'s name and nothing else.'],
         ['sav', 'hard', 'Notice what doesn\'t fit', 'The petrol was bought on a fuel card, not cash. Lea Boys don\'t have fuel cards.', 'Nothing. Just a kid and a mess.'],
         ['btl', 'medium', 'Keep your head when the sirens start in the distance', 'You clear the yard and the lad before anyone with a uniform arrives.', 'You freeze for one long second and the first area car sees you.']]}
    ],
    close: 'At dawn the yard smells of wet ash and petrol. Pat sits on the step with a cup of tea, ledger on her knees, looking at each of you in turn. "Somebody told him where I keep this," she says. "Somebody I trust." She doesn\'t accuse anyone. She doesn\'t have to.',
    notes: ['The grass is a slow-burn question for Missions 8, 11 and 12. Don\'t name them yet. Let the players suspect.',
      'Fire matters: a lit petrol bomb against the shutter uses the Petrol Bomb hazard. Spanner can put it out with the extinguisher if someone buys him a round.',
      'Sending a message back to the Lea Boys is the third objective: let the players choose (a visit to Leyton, a bike returned with a note, a whisper to Vale). Each choice changes Mission 10.',
      'Police arrive after three rounds of gunfire. Otherwise this is a quiet war, and that suits everyone.']
  },
  m05: {
    brief: 'Pat introduces the crew to Big Den at Bow Breakers Yard. He wants a car (a hot hatch, an estate, a hybrid, anything that sells for parts) and he wants it today. Cars are a step up: harder locks, plates, ANPR cameras. The twist: the car they choose belongs to one of Uncle\'s regulars, which sets up Mission 6.',
    open: 'Bow Breakers Yard on Roman Road is a canyon of crushed cars stacked three high, with a Portakabin office at the bottom like a prison guard\'s hut. Big Den comes out wiping his hands on a rag the colour of night. He looks you up and down. "Pat says you\'re good. Pat says that about everyone. Hot hatch, estate, hybrid. Today. Plates off, keys in, out my yard."',
    cast: ['big-den', 'uncle-sol'],
    people: {'big-den': {name: 'Big Den', role: 'Bow Breakers Yard, Roman Road', img: 'systems/the-smoke/assets/tokens/people/ped-17.webp'}},
    roles: {'big-den': 'Buys the car for parts, cash from a biscuit tin.', 'uncle-sol': 'Off stage: the car belongs to one of his regulars.'},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'Street', title: 'Choosing a motor', read: 'Every street has a car worth having if you look properly. A Fiester ST with a cracked bumper outside a betting shop. A Volvagen estate with a child seat and a parking ticket. A Pious hybrid, engine silent, owner nowhere. And on the corner of Bethnal Green Road, a black Brixton Bruiser that someone has clearly spent a lot of money on.',
       text: 'Car locks are Medium to Hard. A relay box or a key programmer (Uncle sells them from Rep 3) makes them two bands easier. Nicking a car is one star at most, even if seen. The Brixton Bruiser belongs to Uncle\'s regular. If the players ask around first, someone mentions it.',
       checks: [['grf', 'hard', 'Get into the car (relay box or key programmer: two bands easier)', 'In, wired, plates in the footwell.', 'The alarm goes. You run or you sit tight and hope.'],
         ['sav', 'medium', 'Ask around before you choose', 'The Bruiser belongs to one of Uncle\'s regulars. Maybe not that one.', 'You don\'t find out until Uncle tells you.']]},
      {at: 'Roads', title: 'The drive to Bow', read: 'Every junction has a camera on a pole. Every pole is a question. On the A11 an area car pulls out of a side road two cars behind you and sits there, patient as a cat.',
       text: 'A nicked car has a plate: drive past a patrol and ANPR pings it (one star). Back streets, a respray, or spare plates keep it quiet. Bow Breakers is on the Old Ford & Bow map.',
       checks: [['whl', 'medium', 'Take the back streets and lose the area car', 'You turn off by the canal and it doesn\'t follow.', 'ANPR pings: one star and a tail.'],
         ['btl', 'easy', 'Keep your nerve at the lights with the area car behind', 'You indicate, you wait, you pull away like a citizen.', 'You jump the lights. Now they\'re interested.']]},
      {at: 'EX-BREAKERS', title: 'Big Den\'s offer', read: 'Big Den walks round the car twice, kicks a tyre, pops the bonnet, and lifts the boot carpet to look at the spare. Then he opens a biscuit tin full of notes and starts counting, and you realise he decided the price before he even came out of the hut.',
       text: 'Pull up in the yard and Big Den makes an offer with a Sell button. He pays less for anything hot (with police looking for it). A Front roll can talk him up a little.',
       checks: [['frt', 'medium', 'Haggle with Big Den', 'He adds a few notes and a grunt of respect.', 'He takes twenty off for wasting his time.']]}
    ],
    close: 'The car disappears into the crusher queue before you have reached the gate. Big Den is already on the phone, reading out a list of parts to somebody in Essex. As you leave, he calls after you without looking up: "Tell Pat. Same again Thursday." Somewhere in Bethnal Green, one of Uncle\'s regulars is staring at an empty parking space.',
    notes: ['This mission upgrades the crew from bikes to cars. Make the difference felt: harder locks, plates, cameras.',
      'If they took the Brixton Bruiser, Uncle\'s regular is upset and Uncle "would like a word" (Mission 6 opens with that).',
      'Pat\'s Rep 9 car jobs (from 0.5.25) send cars to Bow Breakers too: this is where they will come back to.',
      'Big Den is fair and impossible to bully. Trying makes the Yard a place that doesn\'t answer the phone.']
  },
  m06: {
    brief: 'Uncle runs a café in Bethnal Green with two menus. A stallholder on Roman Road Market owes him £500, and Uncle would like it collected politely. The stallholder\'s story (a sick daughter, a robbed stall) is partly true. The crew can lean, haggle, believe him, or pay it themselves for the favour. On the way back there is a pickpocket, and losing Uncle\'s money is a bad start with Uncle.',
    open: 'Uncle\'s café smells of fried eggs, strong tea and money. The menu on the wall says full English £6.50. There is another menu, which isn\'t written down. Uncle sits at the back table in a white shirt and a gold watch, the only customer not eating. He pours you a tea without asking. "A man on Roman Road owes me five hundred pounds, my son. I would like it back. Politely, if possible. If not possible, also politely."',
    cast: ['uncle-sol'],
    roles: {'uncle-sol': 'The client. Courteous, terrifying, keeps a mental ledger of every favour.'},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'Roman Road Market', title: 'The stallholder', read: 'Roman Road Market: phone cases, net curtains, a man selling three pineapples for two pounds at the top of his voice. The stallholder Uncle described is packing up early, an hour before anyone else. When he sees you coming he closes his eyes, just for a second, like a man hearing the dentist\'s drill.',
       text: 'He has a story: his daughter is ill, his stall was robbed last month, he will have it next week. Half of it is true (the robbery was real; the daughter is fine). The sealed £500 envelope is real mission property on the market (collect it to tick the objective).',
       checks: [['frt', 'medium', 'Get the truth out of him kindly', 'The robbery was real; he can pay £300 now and the rest Friday. Uncle will respect that.', 'He cries. You don\'t know whether to believe him.'],
         ['sav', 'medium', 'Spot what\'s really going on', 'The envelope is already made up in his apron; he was paying someone else first.', 'You miss the envelope until he tries to hand you half.'],
         ['btl|mus', 'medium', 'Lean on him (Bottle to look like you mean it, Muscle to show him your size)', 'He hands over the envelope without another word.', 'A stallholder next door calls the police. One star.']]},
      {at: 'Roman Road', title: 'Getting back', read: 'The market crowd folds round you like a tide. A lad bumps your shoulder, says sorry, keeps walking. Your hand goes to your pocket out of habit. The envelope is there. Then, three steps later, it isn\'t.',
       text: 'GM Screen → Street → Pickpocket. A dipper works the crowd. If they lose the envelope, they must get it back before Uncle\'s café closes.',
       checks: [['sav', 'hard', 'Feel the dip as it happens', 'You catch his wrist with the envelope halfway out.', 'Gone. He\'s already ten metres away.'],
         ['mov', 'medium', 'Chase the dipper through the market', 'You catch him at the canal bridge. He hands it back and says sorry again.', 'He vaults a stall and vanishes. Uncle will hear about this.']]}
    ],
    close: 'Uncle opens the envelope, counts the notes once, and slides it into a drawer you did not know the table had. "Good." He pours another tea. Then he turns over the laminated menu on the table. On the back, in small careful handwriting, is a second menu. There are no prices on it. "From today," Uncle says, "you may order from this side."',
    notes: ['Paying the debt yourself is a fine choice: Uncle notices, and the stallholder becomes an ally at Roman Road Market.',
      'Uncle\'s "other menu" opens after this; from Rep 3 the room out the back (shooters) opens too.',
      'If the car from Mission 5 was one of his regulars\', Uncle mentions it here, mildly, which is worse than shouting.',
      'Losing the envelope and not getting it back means the job fails. Uncle gives one more chance, at a price.']
  },
  m07: {
    brief: 'A Range Ruler SUV waits in a car park off Kingsland Road. It has a tracker, the owner has friends in the police, and Kwik Spray closes in an hour. The tracker is not the owner\'s: it is DS Vale\'s, so the respray is the only way to make the car disappear before she sees where it goes. This is the first time Vale is directly on their tail.',
    open: 'The car park off Kingsland Road is half empty and lit like an operating theatre. The Range Ruler sits in the middle of it, black, clean, smug. Pat\'s voice is in your ear on the Blower: "There\'s a tracker. Kwik Spray closes in an hour. And darling? The owner\'s brother-in-law is a police inspector. So don\'t be clever. Be quick."',
    cast: ['auntie-pat', 'harriet-vale'],
    roles: {'auntie-pat': 'Ordered the car; pays for the respray, eventually.', 'harriet-vale': 'Her tracker is in the car, and her unmarked car is the tail.'},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'Kingsland Road car park', title: 'The Range Ruler', read: 'Up close the Range Ruler is spotless. Somebody has had it valeted this week. The tracker could be anywhere: under the wheel arch, behind the bumper, in the boot lining. Across the road a plain silver saloon is parked with its engine running and two people in the front not talking to each other.',
       text: 'Pick up the car (Graft against a Hard lock; a key programmer makes it Easy). The moment they drive off, set Heat to 2: the silver saloon is an unmarked car and it follows.',
       checks: [['grf', 'hard', 'Get in and start it (key programmer: Easy)', 'Engine purring, out of the car park.', 'Alarm, flashing lights, and the silver saloon\'s doors open.'],
         ['grf', 'medium', 'Find the tracker before you go', 'Under the rear bumper: a police-issue unit. Now you know whose it is.', 'You don\'t find it. They\'ll follow you to Kwik Spray.'],
         ['sav', 'medium', 'Clock the silver saloon', 'Unmarked. Two coppers. You plan your route before you turn the key.', 'You only spot it at the first lights, right behind you.']]},
      {at: 'Haggerston streets', title: 'Losing the tail', read: 'The silver saloon hangs three cars back like a bad smell. When you go left, it goes left. When you jump an amber light, it jumps it too, then pretends it didn\'t.',
       text: 'Lose the Police or Lose a Tail. The respray only clears Heat if no unit sees them go into Kwik Spray (Haggerston & London Fields map).',
       checks: [['whl', 'hard', 'Lose the unmarked car', 'Through the estate, out the far side, and it\'s gone.', 'It\'s still there. Now it\'s calling for help.'],
         ['btl', 'medium', 'Sit tight in the respray booth while a patrol rolls past', 'They look straight at the shutter and keep driving.', 'You panic and peek. They see a face.']]},
      {at: 'Kwik Spray', title: 'Kwik Spray Bodyshop', read: 'Kwik Spray is two railway arches knocked together, fumes so thick you can see them. The owner is pulling down the shutter as you arrive. "We\'re closed," he says, and then he sees the car, and then he sees your face, and then he sighs and pulls the shutter back up.',
       text: 'The respray (a local service) changes the plates and clears Heat if no police saw them go in. It costs money; Pat pays it back eventually.',
       checks: [['frt', 'easy', 'Talk the owner into one more job', 'He does it, and throws in new plates.', 'He does it, and charges you double for the inconvenience.']]}
    ],
    close: 'Forty minutes later the Range Ruler is midnight blue with a plate from Essex. You deliver it to Bow Breakers without a single blue light. Behind you, on a tired detective\'s desk in Stoke Newington, a little red dot on a screen has stopped moving outside a bodyshop in Haggerston, and stays stopped all night.',
    notes: ['The tracker is Vale\'s. If the crew find and dump it somewhere funny (on a bus, in the canal), Vale notices and remembers them by name in Mission 8.',
      'The respray is the clever play: it clears Heat only if no unit sees them go in. Make them earn it.',
      'Kwik Spray is on the Haggerston & London Fields map. Respray prices come from Local services.',
      'Owner\'s brother-in-law is a police inspector (Gavin Holt, later in the Lantern Desk career). Seed it.']
  },
  m08: {
    brief: 'DS Harriet Vale picks one PC up on a "routine matter" and buys them a coffee in an interview room. She knows about The Arches. She wants the Lea Boys, or Pat. Each failed Don\'t Grass lets something slip; two and she has enough for a raid (Mission 11 comes sooner and harder). She lets slip on purpose that she already has a grass inside The Chainring.',
    open: 'It happens on Mare Street outside the Co-op, in broad daylight, very politely. A woman in a charcoal suit under a tired raincoat falls into step beside you and shows you a warrant card so briefly it could be a library card. "Detective Sergeant Vale. Nothing to worry about. Routine matter. I\'ve got a car round the corner and I\'ve bought you a coffee." She hands it over. It\'s exactly how you like it.',
    cast: ['harriet-vale'],
    roles: {'harriet-vale': 'Runs the interview. Wants a name, any name. Has one already.'},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'Stoke Newington police station', title: 'The interview room', read: 'The interview room smells of radiator dust and old arguments. The recorder isn\'t on. That is the first thing you notice. The second is the photo face down on the table between you. Vale doesn\'t turn it over. She just taps it, every so often, like a metronome.',
       text: 'Run it with the GM Screen: Police → DS Vale Drops By, then Checks → Don\'t Grass (Hard). Each failure lets something slip (a street, a name, a day of the week). Two failures and Vale has enough to raid The Arches.',
       checks: [['btl', 'hard', 'No comment (Don\'t Grass), every time she asks', 'She learns nothing and respects you for it.', 'Something slips: a day, a street, a first name.'],
         ['sav', 'medium', 'Work out what she already knows', 'She knows about the bikes. She doesn\'t know about the Storehouse. She\'s guessing about Pat.', 'You assume she knows everything and say too much defending something she didn\'t know.'],
         ['frt', 'hard', 'Play her: feed her something useless that sounds true', 'She writes it down. She\'ll waste a week on it.', 'She smiles. She knows exactly what you\'re doing.']]},
      {at: 'Interview room', title: 'The photo', read: 'At the end she turns the photo over. It\'s grainy CCTV: The Arches at night, the shutter half up, a figure inside. She lets you look at it for a long time. "Someone in your little family talks to me," she says. "I thought you should know. It might make you careful. It might make you angry. Either is useful to me."',
       text: 'Vale lets the grass\'s existence slip on purpose: she wants the crew paranoid. The photo is real (it shows the night of Mission 4).',
       checks: [['grt', 'medium', 'Sit out the eight hours she keeps you "helping with enquiries"', 'You walk out at midnight looking bored.', 'Knackered and rattled; Shaken for the rest of the day.']]}
    ],
    close: 'They let you go at midnight through the side door, into rain. No charge, no caution, no record that you were ever there. Your Blower has twenty-three missed calls from Pat. You stand in the doorway of the kebab shop opposite the station for a while before you call her back, deciding what to say.',
    notes: ['Giving her the Lea Boys makes Mission 12 easier (half of Stretch\'s crew is arrested), but if Pat finds out (Savvy against the player\'s Front), the crew turns cold.',
      'Two failed Don\'t Grass rolls: Mission 11 (the raid) happens sooner and harder, and Vale knows about the back door.',
      'Covering their tracks before Pat hears is the third objective: let them choose what to tell Pat. Lying to Pat is a Front roll she gets to contest with Savvy.',
      'Say nothing at a police stop (Bottle) is now a real option too: this interview is the same skill in a smaller room.']
  },
  m09: {
    brief: 'A sports shop in Westfield Stratford takes a limited trainer drop on Thursday and Uncle has buyers for every pair. Smash, grab, mopeds, gone. Casing it first earns facts that make the grab easier. The moment the glass goes it is two stars (three if a guard is hurt), Westfield is wide open, and the helicopter comes at three. The twist: Stretch\'s lads are doing the same shop the same night.',
    open: 'Uncle slides a phone across the table. On the screen: a pair of trainers so white they look radioactive, and a number with a lot of zeros. "Thursday," he says. "Westfield. The shop takes delivery at eight in the evening. They put them in the window to be photographed. I have a buyer for every pair, my son. Every single pair."',
    cast: ['uncle-sol', 'stretch', 'lea-boy'],
    roles: {'uncle-sol': 'Has the buyers and the plan. Wants it done without blood.', stretch: 'His lads are hitting the same shop, the same night.', 'lea-boy': 'Four of them on mopeds, arriving at the same moment.'},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'ST1.1', title: 'Casing the shop', read: 'Westfield on a weekday afternoon: music from nowhere, a man selling phone cases from a kiosk, a security guard eating a sandwich on the bench outside the sports shop with his radio turned down. The shutter is the fast kind. The camera above the door is the old kind. One of them is pointing at the wrong place.',
       text: 'Case the Joint: one useful fact per success (the guard\'s break at 7:40, the shutter speed, the broken camera, the fire exit by the bins). Each fact makes the grab one band easier.',
       checks: [['sav', 'medium', 'Case the joint (one fact per margin point)', 'Facts: the guard\'s break, the dead camera, the bin-store exit.', 'You learn nothing you couldn\'t have guessed.'],
         ['frt', 'medium', 'Chat up the guard on his bench', 'He tells you the shutter sticks halfway on cold nights.', 'He remembers your face on Thursday.']]},
      {at: 'ST1.1', title: 'The grab', read: 'Thursday, 8:03pm. The trainers are in the window, lit like a shrine. Somebody inside is photographing them for social media. Then four mopeds come screaming down the mall from the other end, and you see the tracksuits, and you realise you are not the only people who read the same message board.',
       text: 'The grab is a teamwork check. The moment the glass goes, Heat 2 (3 if a guard is hurt). Stretch\'s lads are here for the same stock. The trainers are one physical parcel to collect.',
       checks: [['mus', 'teamwork', 'The grab: through the glass, bags filled, out', 'In and out in ninety seconds with most of the drop.', 'Half the bags, a cut hand, and the guard on his radio.'],
         ['grf', 'hard', 'Jam the shutter so the Lea Boys can\'t follow you in', 'It comes down behind you. Stretch\'s lads hammer on the outside.', 'It sticks halfway. Now everyone\'s inside.'],
         ['aim', 'medium', 'Spray the camera on the way in', 'Black lens. No faces on the footage.', 'You miss; your face is on the evening news.']]},
      {at: 'Stratford roads', title: 'Getting out of Stratford', read: 'Outside, Stratford is all wide roads and open plazas, built for crowds and terrible for hiding. Somewhere over the Olympic Park a helicopter is turning towards the noise.',
       text: 'Get out of Stratford with three stars or less, and drop the goods at the flat (physical delivery). Wide roads, few hiding places; the helicopter comes at three stars.',
       checks: [['whl', 'hard', 'Mopeds out through the bus lanes', 'You\'re across the Lea before the first unit arrives.', 'An area car on your back wheel and the helicopter overhead.'],
         ['mov', 'medium', 'Ditch the bike and walk out with the shoppers', 'Just another bag in a crowd.', 'A shop assistant points you out.']]}
    ],
    close: 'The flat is full of shoeboxes. Every single pair. Uncle\'s buyer collects them at midnight in an unmarked van and leaves an envelope on the kitchen table without coming inside. On the local news, a police spokesman describes "two rival groups" and "disgraceful scenes", and somewhere in Leyton, Stretch is watching the same report and working out who beat him to it.',
    notes: ['This is the first big score. Heat rules matter here: two stars when the glass goes, three if a guard is hurt.',
      'The Lea Boys arriving at the same time turns a heist into a three-way scramble. Let them fight over the bags or make a deal in the middle of the mall.',
      'If anyone hurts the guard, Uncle\'s buyer cancels. Uncle does not do blood.',
      'The dead camera (if they found it) means no faces on the news. If not, their Heat cools slower this week.']
  },
  m10: {
    brief: 'Stretch texts a challenge: a race across Hackney Marshes at dusk. Winner takes the patch west of the Lea; loser gives up their bike. Three legs (the football pitches, the canal bridge, the Lea towpath), each an opposed Wheels roll. Stretch cheats: a Lea Boy with a bike chain waits at the bridge.',
    open: 'The text arrives at 6:12pm with a pin on the map: Hackney Marshes, the North Pavilion. "Dusk. You vs me. Pitches, bridge, towpath. Winner takes west of the Lea. Loser walks home." Then a second text, a minute later: "Bring a proper bike. Not one of Pat\'s."',
    cast: ['stretch', 'lea-boy', 'kez'],
    roles: {stretch: 'Races, and cheats.', 'lea-boy': 'Waits at the canal bridge with a bike chain.', kez: 'Comes along to watch, and can be talked into riding the second leg.'},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'Hackney Marshes', title: 'The start line', read: 'Hackney Marshes at dusk: eighty football pitches going grey in the half-light, goalposts like gallows, the Lea a silver line in the distance. Stretch sits on a Dirt Devil scrambler with the engine ticking. A crowd of Lea Boys on mopeds have their headlights on the start line. Somebody has brought a speaker. Somebody else has brought a flare.',
       text: 'Three legs, each an opposed Wheels roll against Stretch (GM Screen → Checks → Opposed). Off-road bikes get +1 on the pitches.',
       checks: [['whl', 'opposed', 'Leg one: across the football pitches (off-road +1)', 'You\'re ahead at the first goalposts.', 'Stretch is gone in a spray of mud.'],
         ['sav', 'medium', 'Pick the line: you know the Marshes', 'You find the hard path along the ditch: +1 on the next leg.', 'You hit the boggy pitch everyone avoids.']]},
      {at: 'Canal bridge', title: 'The bridge', read: 'At the canal bridge a lad steps out of the bushes swinging a bike chain at head height, and the world goes slow.',
       text: 'The cheat. Dodge it (Moves), take it (Grit), or knock him off (Muscle). Then leg two, opposed Wheels.',
       checks: [['mov|grt', 'medium', 'The bike chain: duck it (Moves) or ride through it (Grit)', 'Past him, and you heard the chain whistle over your head.', 'It catches you. Hurt, and you\'ve lost a leg of the race.'],
         ['whl', 'opposed', 'Leg two: over the bridge', 'You take the bridge first.', 'He\'s ahead, laughing.']]},
      {at: 'Lea towpath', title: 'The towpath', read: 'The towpath is a ribbon of gravel between a hedge and black water. Narrowboats moored along it with their curtains closed. Stretch is a headlight in your mirror, or a tail-light ahead of you, and the finish is a flare burning red on the far bank.',
       text: 'Leg three, opposed Wheels. A Proper Mess on the towpath: Come Off the Bike, and the canal is right there.',
       checks: [['whl', 'opposed', 'Leg three: the towpath sprint', 'You cross the flare first.', 'He does.'],
         ['grt', 'medium', 'If you go in the canal: get out', 'Soaked and freezing, but you\'re out.', 'Knackered and the bike\'s at the bottom.']]}
    ],
    close: 'Whoever wins, the Marshes go quiet afterwards. The Lea Boys\' headlights swing away one by one. Stretch rolls up alongside you with his engine ticking and looks at you for a long time. "Fair," he says, eventually, which is the closest he will ever come to respect. Or he doesn\'t say anything at all, and that is worse.',
    notes: ['Winning: Stretch loses face and the patch west of the Lea is the crew\'s (Pat is delighted). Losing: Pat loses a patch and somebody walks home.',
      'Calling out the cheat publicly (a PC who spots the chain lad before the race, Savvy Hard) shames Stretch in front of his own crew.',
      'Either way, Mission 12 is coming. Stretch now knows exactly what the crew can do.',
      'The Marshes are wide open: no police unless someone fires a flare at a copper.']
  },
  m11: {
    brief: 'Five in the morning, Kez rings: three police vans on Morning Lane, a dog unit, and DS Vale with a warrant. Pat is still inside with the ledger. Get Pat out the back, save the ledger, lose the Heat. If Mission 8 went badly, Vale knows about the back door. The twist: the ledger names the grass, and it is someone the players like.',
    open: 'The phone. 5:02am. Kez, breathless, whispering: "Vans. Three of them. Morning Lane. Dogs. And her, Vale, with a bit of paper. Pat\'s inside. She won\'t leave without the book." In the background a dog is barking like it\'s been waiting for this all its life.',
    cast: ['auntie-pat', 'harriet-vale', 'kez', 'spanner-rob'],
    roles: {'auntie-pat': 'Inside with the ledger. Won\'t leave it.', 'harriet-vale': 'Leads the raid, by the book.', kez: 'Watching from the railway embankment.', 'spanner-rob': 'Inside, stripping bikes as fast as he can, so there\'s nothing to find.'},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'Morning Lane', title: 'The vans', read: 'Blue lights wash the arches. Officers in black spill from three carriers onto Morning Lane, one with a battering ram, two with a dog straining at its lead. DS Vale stands by the first van in her raincoat, reading something on her phone, completely calm. She looks up the street, straight at where you\'re standing, and smiles like you\'re late for a meeting.',
       text: 'Set Heat 3 for anyone seen near the yard. Police → Police Dog and Hazards → CS Spray for officers at the shutter. Officers take their own turns in the Combat Console. Surrendering is an option: Busted, but the ledger might be safe if someone else has it.',
       checks: [['mov', 'hard', 'Get to the back door over the railway embankment', 'Over the fence and in through the back before the ram hits the front.', 'The dog sees you first.'],
         ['btl', 'medium', 'Freeze for the police dog (or run)', 'It sniffs you, bored, and moves on.', 'It goes for your arm.']]},
      {at: 'AR4', title: 'Inside: Pat and the ledger', read: 'Inside, Pat is sitting at her desk with the ledger open, calmly tearing out pages and feeding them into a mug of tea. "Not all of it, darlings," she says. "Some of it\'s sentimental." The front shutter shudders under the first blow of the ram.',
       text: 'Search the Place (teamwork) to get the ledger and anything else incriminating. The ledger is real mission property: someone has to carry it out and get it to the flat.',
       checks: [['sav', 'teamwork', 'Search the place: the ledger, the cash, the bits that matter', 'Everything that matters in one bag.', 'You leave something behind. Vale will find it.'],
         ['mus', 'hard', 'Hold the shutter while the others work', 'Three more blows. Three more seconds each.', 'It goes. Officers flood in.'],
         ['grt', 'medium', 'Keep going through the CS spray', 'Eyes streaming, you keep moving.', 'Blind and coughing: Gassed.']]},
      {at: 'AR5', title: 'Out the back', read: 'The back door opens onto the railway embankment and a fence topped with wire. On the other side, an empty lane and the overground line. Somewhere above, a train is coming, and the whole arch begins to hum.',
       text: 'Getting Pat over the fence (she is sixty-something, in a cardigan). The ledger can go over first.',
       checks: [['aim', 'medium', 'Throw the ledger over the fence to Kez', 'It lands in his arms. He\'s gone on the BMX before anyone looks up.', 'It hits the wire and flaps there like a dying bird.'],
         ['mus|mov', 'medium', 'Get Pat over the fence (lift her, or find the gap)', 'Over and away, cardigan only slightly torn.', 'She snags. An officer comes round the side.']]}
    ],
    close: 'Later, in your flat with the curtains drawn, Pat sits at your kitchen table with the ledger, turning pages with a licked finger. She stops on one, reads it twice, and goes very still. Then she turns the book round and slides it across to you. A name, in her own handwriting, next to a column of payments from someone who isn\'t her. Someone you like.',
    notes: ['The grass is your choice: pick a well-liked NPC (Kez, Spanner, Mo, even Big Sal). The ledger proves it. Mission 12 ends with what Pat does about it.',
      'If Mission 8 went badly, Vale has the back door covered: two officers waiting at the fence.',
      'Surrender is a valid choice: Busted (fine and custody), but if another PC got the ledger out, the crew still wins.',
      'The ledger delivered to the flat ticks the delivery objective.']
  },
  m12: {
    brief: 'The finale of the opening. Pat has found Stretch\'s stock: a lock-up behind the Leyton Orient ground, two hundred bikes, a van of parts, and Stretch\'s cash box. Getting in, the fight inside, a convoy home, and then Pat, the ledger and the grass. DS Vale has been watching the lock-up too, and she lets them go, this time.',
    open: 'Pat calls you to The Arches at midnight. The shutter is half down, the lights off, everyone talking in whispers. She unfolds a map of Leyton on the workbench and puts her finger on a row of lock-ups behind the football ground. "Two hundred bikes," she says. "A van of parts. And his cash box. Tonight, darlings, it ends. One way or another."',
    cast: ['auntie-pat', 'stretch', 'lea-boy', 'harriet-vale'],
    roles: {'auntie-pat': 'Plans it, comes along, and decides the grass\'s fate.', stretch: 'Inside the lock-up with four lads and a dog.', 'lea-boy': 'Four of them, and one has the cash box.', 'harriet-vale': 'Watching from an unmarked car. Lets them go.'},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'LY1.6', title: 'Getting in: the lock-ups behind the ground', read: 'Behind the Leyton Orient ground, a row of garages with corrugated roofs and graffiti nobody has tagged over in years. Number fourteen has a new padlock, a new shutter, and a strip of light underneath it. Somewhere inside, a dog barks once, then stops, like someone told it to.',
       text: 'Getting in: Pick a Lock, Break Down a Door, or ram the shutter with a vehicle. Killing the security light first helps.',
       checks: [['aim', 'medium', 'Kill the security light (a brick, or a shot if you want the noise)', 'Darkness. They won\'t see you coming.', 'Glass everywhere and the dog going mad.'],
         ['grf|mus', 'hard', 'The shutter: pick the padlock (Graft) or rip it open (Muscle)', 'Up and in before they reach the light switch.', 'It holds. They know you\'re here.']]},
      {at: 'LY1.6', title: 'Inside lock-up 14', read: 'Bikes, hundreds of them, hung from the ceiling on hooks like meat in a cold store. A van with its back doors open, full of parts. Stretch standing on a workbench in the middle of it all with a cricket bat, four of his lads around him and a dog straining at a chain. "Took your time," he says.',
       text: 'Use the Combat Console: Stretch, four Lea Boys and a guard dog. Police arrive at Heat 3 after three rounds of gunfire; Trojans at four.',
       checks: [['mus', 'medium', 'The fight (Combat Console)', 'Stretch goes down and stays down.', 'Somebody\'s hurt, and the cash box is moving.'],
         ['frt|btl', 'hard', 'Or talk Stretch into giving it up (Front to persuade, Bottle to outlast him)', 'He gives you the cash box and the van, and keeps his pride and his bike.', 'He laughs. "Nah." The dog\'s off the chain.']]},
      {at: 'Leyton to Hackney', title: 'The convoy home', read: 'The van, nose down under the weight of parts, bikes strapped to the back like a cycle shop in an earthquake. Three in the cab, the rest riding alongside. Across the road an unmarked car sits with its lights off, and the face in the driver\'s seat is lit by a phone screen.',
       text: 'The stock crate is physical mission property; deliver it to The Arches. Bow Breakers will take the van; Pat takes the bikes. DS Vale watches and does nothing.',
       checks: [['whl', 'medium', 'Drive the overloaded van home without losing anything', 'Every bike, every part, home by three.', 'A crate slides off at the Lea Bridge roundabout.'],
         ['grt', 'medium', 'Keep going (it\'s 3am, you\'re bleeding, it\'s raining)', 'You make it.', 'Knackered, and somebody else has to drive.']]}
    ],
    close: 'Back at The Arches, as the sky goes grey, Pat sits with the ledger on her knee and the grass in the chair opposite. The whole crew is watching. She turns to you. "You found it," she says. "You decide." When it is done, whatever is done, she puts the kettle on, because she always puts the kettle on. Outside, a train goes over, and Hackney wakes up like nothing happened. It isn\'t the end. It\'s the start of whatever you want The Smoke to be.',
    notes: ['This ends "On Your Bike". Let the players decide the grass\'s fate. Forgiveness, exile or worse all shape what the Chainring becomes in the Sandbox Campaign.',
      'DS Vale lets them go. Tell them she was there. She will come back in the Lantern Desk career.',
      'Bow Breakers buys the van; Pat buys the bikes. With Rep 9, Pat\'s car jobs open (from 0.5.25).',
      'From here, open the Mission Board\'s faction careers. The Chainring\'s CH4 picks up the threads.']
  }
};
